\label{ch:calling-conventions}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:calling-conv-intro}

A calling convention is a protocol governing how subroutines are called and how they return. It specifies which registers hold arguments, where return values are placed, which registers the caller must preserve, and how the stack is organized during a call. The calling convention is the most visible aspect of the \emph{application binary interface} (ABI)---the contract between independently compiled code units.

The calling convention must be agreed upon by all compilers and libraries that produce code for a given platform. If two compilers disagree on the calling convention, code compiled by one cannot correctly call functions compiled by the other. This makes calling conventions a matter of standardization, not just implementation.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of calling conventions, from the fundamental design choices to the specifics of major platform ABIs.

\section{Argument Passing: Registers vs.\ Stack}\label{sec:arg-passing}

\subsection{The Design Space}\label{sec:arg-design-space}

The fundamental question in calling convention design is: how are arguments passed from caller to callee?

\begin{definition}[Argument Passing Mechanisms]\label{def:arg-passing}
Arguments can be passed via:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Registers}: the caller places arguments in designated registers before the call.
  \item \textbf{Stack}: the caller pushes arguments onto the stack before the call.
  \item \textbf{Hybrid}: the first $k$ arguments go in registers; overflow arguments go on the stack.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Trade-offs in argument passing}
\label{tab:arg-passing}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Registers} & \textbf{Stack} \\
\hline
Speed & Fast (no memory access) & Slower (memory access) \\
Code size & Compact & Larger (push instructions) \\
Simplicity & Complex (register allocation) & Simple (uniform) \\
Variadic support & Difficult & Natural \\
Recursion & No extra work & Natural \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Most modern ABIs use the hybrid approach: registers for the common case (few arguments), stack for the general case (many arguments or large arguments).

\subsection{Register Argument Classification}\label{sec:arg-classification}

Arguments are classified by type and size to determine which register class they use:

\begin{definition}[Argument Classification]\label{def:arg-class}
Each argument type is classified into a \emph{register class}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{INTEGER}: integer types, pointers, and small structures containing only integer fields.
  \item \textbf{SSE}/\textbf{VECTOR}: floating-point types and SIMD vectors.
  \item \textbf{MEMORY}: large types or types with complex structure that must be passed on the stack.
  \item \textbf{COMPLEX}: types that span multiple register classes (e.g., a struct with both integer and float fields).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The System V AMD64 ABI, for example, classifies each eightbyte (8-byte chunk) of an argument independently. A struct with two doubles is classified as two SSE eightbytes; a struct with an int and a double is classified as one INTEGER and one SSE eightbyte.

\subsection{Stack Argument Layout}\label{sec:stack-arg-layout}

When arguments overflow to the stack, their layout must be specified:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Order}: arguments are pushed right-to-left (C convention) or left-to-right.
  \item \textbf{Alignment}: each argument may require specific alignment on the stack.
  \item \textbf{Padding}: gaps may be inserted between arguments for alignment.
  \item \textbf{Home space}: some ABIs reserve stack space for register arguments (the ``shadow space'' or ``home area'') so that callees can spill register arguments to a known location.
\end{itemize}

\section{Return Value Conventions}\label{sec:return-values}

\subsection{Single Return Values}\label{sec:single-return}

The simplest case: a function returns a single value.

\begin{definition}[Return Value Placement]\label{def:return-value}
The return value is placed in a designated register:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Integer/pointer types: \texttt{RAX} (x86-64), \texttt{X0} (ARM64), \texttt{a0} (RISC-V).
  \item Floating-point types: \texttt{XMM0} (x86-64), \texttt{D0} (ARM64), \texttt{fa0} (RISC-V).
  \item Large types: returned via a hidden pointer argument (the caller allocates space and passes its address).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Multiple Return Values}\label{sec:multiple-return}

Some languages support functions that return multiple values. The ABI must specify how these are placed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pair in registers}: two integer values in \texttt{RAX}/\texttt{RDX}, two floats in \texttt{XMM0}/\texttt{XMM1}.
  \item \textbf{Hidden pointer}: the caller allocates a struct and passes a pointer; the callee fills it in.
  \item \textbf{Stack return}: the return value is placed on the stack (rare in modern ABIs).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Struct Return}\label{sec:struct-return}

Structures that do not fit in the return registers require special handling:

\begin{definition}[Struct Return Convention]\label{def:struct-return}
When a function returns a struct too large for the return registers:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The caller allocates space for the return value.
  \item The caller passes a \emph{hidden first argument} (a pointer to this space).
  \item The callee writes the return value to this location.
  \item The callee returns the pointer (in \texttt{RAX} on x86-64) for chaining.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\section{Caller-Saved vs.\ Callee-Saved Registers}\label{sec:save-conventions}

\subsection{The Preservation Question}\label{sec:preservation-question}

When a function is called, some registers may be overwritten by the callee. The question is: who is responsible for preserving the values that survive across the call---the caller or the callee?

\begin{definition}[Caller-Saved (Volatile) Registers]\label{def:caller-saved}
A \emph{caller-saved} (volatile) register may be destroyed by the callee. If the caller needs the value after the call, it must save the register before the call and restore it afterward.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Callee-Saved (Non-Volatile) Registers]\label{def:callee-saved}
A \emph{callee-saved} (non-volatile) register must be preserved by the callee. If the callee uses the register, it must save the original value on entry and restore it before returning.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Design Considerations}\label{sec:save-design}

The choice of which registers are caller-saved vs.\ callee-saved affects code quality:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Many callee-saved registers}: fewer saves around calls, but each function that uses them must save/restore. Good for functions with few calls but many local variables.
  \item \textbf{Many caller-saved registers}: the register allocator has more freedom, but must insert saves around each call. Good for leaf functions and functions with many calls.
  \item \textbf{Mixed}: most ABIs use a mix, balancing the two concerns.
\end{itemize}

