\section{Stack Frame Layout}\label{sec:stack-frame}

\subsection{Activation Records}\label{sec:activation-records}

Each function invocation creates an \emph{activation record} (stack frame) on the call stack. The frame contains:

\begin{definition}[Stack Frame Components]\label{def:stack-frame}
A typical stack frame contains:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Arguments}: passed by the caller (may be in registers or on the stack).
  \item \textbf{Return address}: the address to return to after the function completes.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic link}: pointer to the caller's frame (frame pointer chain).
  \item \textbf{Saved registers}: callee-saved registers that the function uses.
  \item \textbf{Local variables}: the function's local storage.
  \item \textbf{Temporaries}: intermediate values computed during execution.
  \item \textbf{Outgoing arguments}: space for arguments passed to callees.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

